    kind: "succulent", type: "Mat-forming evergreen succulents", badges: ["Evergreen rosettes", "Very drought tolerant", "Cultivars mixed"],
    description: "Houseleeks build a low mosaic of fleshy pointed rosettes among the Stone Bed gravel. Each mature rosette flowers only once, lifting a starry summer cluster before that rosette dies, but surrounding offsets close the gap and continue the colony. Their name Sempervivum means ‘always living’; in a British gravel bed the real threat is not drought but shade, rich soil and winter water trapped inside the rosettes.",
    months: ["Jun", "Jul", "Aug"], size: "5–15cm high", sizeDetail: "Offsets form spreading mats 30cm or more across", position: "Full sun", positionDetail: "Open light keeps rosettes compact and strongly coloured", soil: "Sharply drained and gritty", soilDetail: "Lean alpine conditions; keep crowns above wet soil", hardiness: "Mostly H5–H7", hardinessDetail: "Cold hardy when protected from winter wet", feature: "Geometric evergreen rosettes", featureDetail: "Colours change with cultivar, temperature and season", identity: "Sempervivum collection", identityDetail: "Individual cultivars not recorded",
    under: "wrinkled leaves after an exceptional prolonged drought", over: "translucent leaves and brown rot spreading through a rosette", spring: "Remove dead flowering gaps, re-seat healthy offsets in fresh grit and clear leaf litter from rosette centres.", summer: "Water only newly placed offsets; enjoy flower stalks, then remove the dead parent rosette after bloom.", autumn: "Stop feeding and clear fallen leaves before wet weather settles into the mat.", winter: "Leave dry cold rosettes alone and protect the bed from debris or runoff that holds water around them.",
    problem: { name: "Death after flowering", sign: "One central rosette browns after its flower stalk fades", response: "This is normal monocarpic growth: remove that rosette and guide surrounding offsets into the space." },
    about: "Sempervivum are mountain succulents from Europe and Asia, adapted to exposed rock through water-storing leaves and clonal offsets. Seasonal colour is fluid, so identifying cultivars from a summer photograph is unreliable. The colony is maintained by its younger offsets rather than by keeping every parent rosette alive indefinitely.", provenance: "Genus is secure; the Stone Bed is treated as a mixed houseleek collection without invented cultivar names.", family: "Crassulaceae", genus: "Sempervivum", foliage: "Evergreen and succulent", habit: "Rosette-forming with stoloniferous offsets", role: "Low living mosaic through the gravel", observation: "Multiple rosettes fill dry gaps and maintain colour through winter.", status: "Photograph individual rosettes seasonally before attempting any cultivar identification.", source: RHS.sempervivum,
